[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"DataStore vs SharedPreferences Internals in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"For years, SharedPreferences has been the go-to key-value storage solution in Android. However, Jetpack DataStore has emerged as its modern successor, built on Kotlin Coroutines and Flow. This tutorial dives deep into the internal architecture of both mechanisms to understand why DataStore is safer, more efficient, and better suited for modern Android development."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"SharedPreferences Internals: How It Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"SharedPreferences uses an XML file stored in the application's private data directory (data/data/package_name/shared_prefs/). When you call getSharedPreferences(), the system reads the entire XML file from disk and caches its contents in a HashMap in memory."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Loading: Disk I/O happens synchronously on the calling thread during the initial load if the cache is not yet populated."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Writing (apply): Writes to memory immediately, then asynchronously commits to disk using a single-thread executor."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Writing (commit): Writes synchronously to memory and blocks the calling thread until the disk write is complete."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Flaws in SharedPreferences"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Despite its simplicity, SharedPreferences has major architectural flaws that lead to ANRs (Application Not Responding) and data corruption."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ANR Risks: commit() blocks the main thread. Even apply() can cause ANRs during service or activity teardown because queued writes are forced to run on the main thread."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No Exception Safety: Parsing errors or disk read failures can throw uncaught runtime exceptions."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lack of Safety: It lacks type safety and transactional guarantees, often leading to cast exceptions and race conditions."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Jetpack DataStore Internals: Preferences DataStore"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"DataStore replaces SharedPreferences by using asynchronous, consistent, transactional storage. It comes in two flavors: Preferences DataStore and Proto DataStore. We will focus on Preferences DataStore using Java interoperability."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Asynchronous API: Built on Flows, eliminating blocking disk reads on the main thread."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ACID Transactions: Uses Protocol Buffers and custom readers/writers to ensure atomic writes, preventing partial or corrupted data states."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Preferences DataStore in Java"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use DataStore in a Java-based Android project, you must include the dependency and use RxJava or Kotlin Coroutines interoperability to read and write data."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"// Gradle dependency
implementation 'androidx.datastore:datastore-preferences:1.1.0'
implementation 'androidx.datastore:datastore-preferences-rxjava2:1.1.0' // or rxjava3"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is how you can set up and write data using the RxJava2 extension for DataStore:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"RxDataStore<Preferences> dataStore = new RxPreferenceDataStoreBuilder(context, "settings").build();

Preferences.Key<String> USER_NAME_KEY = PreferencesKeys.stringKey("user_name");

// Writing data asynchronously
Single<Preferences> updateResult = dataStore.updateDataAsync(prefs -> {
    MutablePreferences mutablePreferences = prefs.toMutablePreferences();
    mutablePreferences.set(USER_NAME_KEY, "JohnDoe");
    return Single.just(mutablePreferences);
});"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Reading Data from DataStore"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reading data is exposed as a reactive stream, ensuring that UI components can observe changes safely without polling."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"Flowable<String> userNameFlow = dataStore.data()
    .map(prefs -> prefs.get(USER_NAME_KEY));

userNameFlow.subscribe(name -> {
    // Handle name update
});"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary Comparison"},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Thread Safety: SharedPreferences is thread-safe for reads and writes, but apply() can cause main-thread blockages during lifecycle teardowns. DataStore is fully non-blocking and safe."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Exceptions: SharedPreferences throws runtime exceptions on bad casts or file parsing errors. DataStore wraps IO exceptions safely within reactive error channels."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Migration: DataStore provides SharedPreferencesMigration to easily bridge old data into the new storage format."}]
